These statements directly use `quote_buf->out_len` without validation. A compromised host can set a value larger than the allocated buffer (`TDX_QUOTE_MAX_LEN`), causing `kvmemdup` to read out-of-bounds memory and leak data to userspace. This violates trust boundaries, as the guest expects the host to provide a valid length.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
out_len = READ_ONCE(quote_buf->out_len);
if (out_len > TDX_QUOTE_MAX_LEN)
	return -EFBIG;
```
Reason:
`READ_ONCE` ensures a single atomic read of the host-controlled value, preventing race conditions where the host modifies the length concurrently. The subsequent check rejects any length exceeding the maximum allowed (`TDX_QUOTE_MAX_LEN`). After validation, `out_len` is used in place of the raw `quote_buf->out_len`, ensuring only safe memory accesses occur.

